# Django migration files themselves are NOT ignored: they're schema history and
# must be version-controlled identically to every other environment (dev, CI,
# staging, prod) that runs `manage.py migrate` against this codebase. The
# previous rule here silently excluded every migration matching Django's
# standard 000N_*.py naming from git entirely -- `git add -A`/`git add .`
# after `makemigrations` would report the new file as added but it was
# actually dropped, since gitignore doesn't affect already-tracked files but
# silently blocks newly created ones. That let six apps' models (including
# football_data_app's FootballFixture/Market/Configuration) drift for months
# from what's actually committed, with no error until a fresh checkout (a new
# environment, CI, or a clean redeploy) ran `migrate` against models.py and
# hit missing-column errors for fields the working tree's models.py had had
# all along.
**/migrations/__pycache__/

